#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Instant-budget census: key press or click to the first changed pixel, for every row of the budget.

  flea-field-instant budget                                      the journey table as JSON, the data a gate reads
  flea-field-instant fixtures ROOT [--usb DIR] [--nas DIR]       build every fixture in marked sandbox roots
  flea-field-instant run PART --root ROOT --flea TREE --out DIR [--samples N] [--dry] [--only REGEX] [--frontend gpui|qml] [--bin FILE]   one census part
  flea-field-instant measure PLAN.json                           the same from a plan file: {"part", "root", "flea", "out", "samples", "only"}
  flea-field-instant report JSONL... [--out FILE] [--calibrate FILE]   the census table in Markdown
  flea-field-instant gate JSONL... [--out FILE] [--part PART]... [--only REGEX]   pass or fail against the budget ceilings
  flea-field-instant calibrate [--runs N]                        negative control, 0, 50 and 200 ms, the key repeat count, the fling floor
  flea-field-instant selftest                                    detector, budget, held-sweep and report logic, no display
  flea-field-instant clean ROOT                                  remove the fixture roots ROOT/fixtures.json names

FLEA_INSTANT_PARENT (default /home/flea-sandbox) is where ROOT must live; FLEA_INSTANT_SMALL=1 shrinks the two big
files to a few MiB, for a builder smoke away from minipc.

Parts: smoke, p1-cursor, p2-held, p3-navigate, p4-open, p5-heavy, p6-ops, p7-load, p8-rename (PARTS below).

The method is tools/flea-field-nav's, which is tools/flea-field-search's: tools/flea-framecap records which presented
frames change inside a rectangle, stamped with the compositor's presentation time in CLOCK_MONOTONIC, and every input
(key, button, wheel notch, touchpad stroke) goes to a uinput device stamped in CLOCK_MONOTONIC just before the write.
A sample is the input to the first frame in which the watched region changes with ink (first_ms, the number the
budget judges), beside the first change of any kind (first_any_ms) and the last change before a quiet gap (settled_ms).
A key that changes nothing before the next input is a miss and counts as over every limit, never as a dropped sample.

Ops (row 6). The backend runs one write at a time and refuses a second as an error line, and the status bar draws only the
oldest unacknowledged error, so a paste that starts under a running job or a queued error draws nothing of its own. Every op
sample therefore starts on an idle status bar: statusActivityState (ui/Ipc.qml) is read, Esc dismisses each queued error and
cancels a running transfer, and a bar that is not clear inside 20 s ends the sample with an error row, never a timing. A
paste that does not finish inside its wait (120 s, 900 s for op.copy.long) is cancelled with Esc and given 60 s to leave
the activity list before the next sample. Each op row carries errors_before and errors_after (queued errors), activity_after
(the activities still up), notice_before and notice_after (the status notice before and after the paste), esc_before (Escs
the bar needed before the paste) and refused, true when the paste left more queued errors than it found; the report counts
refused samples beside the misses.
A sample also starts in the folder sample 0 started in: the pane's path, listInFlight, total, cursor and fileClipboard
(ui/Ipc.qml) are read before the take and before the paste, the source and the destination are reached by row name (a symlink
to a folder is listed among the files, after b-src), and a sample that cannot reach them, or whose take holds the wrong
paths, is a harness fault row ("harness fault" in the report), never a refusal or a miss. op.size.recursive runs with the
Properties row shown (menu.hidden ships it hidden).

Fling floor. calibrate plays the census stroke (series_fling's, 40 mm in 120 ms) to tests/search-calib.qml, an idle Qt
client, and reads the stroke's first motion report to the client's first pixel-carrying wheel event (its log, on the wall clock
bridged to CLOCK_MONOTONIC; the client clock floors to a millisecond, so that figure reads up to 1 ms early) and to the
client's first changed frame (the recorder). That is the share of a fling's first_ms that libinput, Hyprland and Qt take
before any Flea code runs. calibrate prints it under "fling" and report prints it beside the fling rows (it reads the
calibrate.json beside its first JSONL, or --calibrate FILE, whose failure to give a floor is said in the note). judge never subtracts it.

Held key (row 2). j is held on a 10k folder for HOLD_REPEATS repeat periods plus half of one, so the release falls in
the middle of a repeat slot. repeats drawn: slots of one repeat period, anchored on the first change after the first
repeat is due, each needing a changed frame. overshoot: Flea's cursor steps between two IPC reads of the cursor index,
one before the press and one after 2 s of quiet, minus the most key events an idle Qt client (tests/search-calib.qml)
received for the same physical hold on the same host (repeat_probe). Qt's repeat timer never fires more often when the
client is busy, so any step past that bound was handled after the release: a backlog cannot pass.

Sample input, one recorder line: "F 660389861257350 660389874933379 32 95 126 300 2400 61" (see tools/flea-framecap.c).
Sample row: {"journey": "nav.list.down", "row": 1, "view": "list", "fixture": "local", "first_ms": 6.1, "settled_ms": 30.2}.
A journey on the USB stick or the NAS share is row 5 whatever its local row; under load it keeps its local row.
Cursor chords are the default preset's (keys.toml [[code]] Down Up Home End PageUp PageDown, [[ctrl]] D U, [[text]]
j k g G); the grid alone steps sideways on the bare arrows and on h and l.
Parts are sessions of fixture, view, region and series from fixtures.json, so --dry checks a part with no display.
--dry also prints, per session, where the pointer goes for a wheel or a stroke and which row an Enter series lands on.

Journey filter. run PART --only REGEX keeps the series with a journey that matches REGEX (re.search over the journey
ids journeys_of lists) and drops a session left with none, so a re-measure of a few journeys costs only those sessions.
A series is kept whole: a cycle that times enter and back runs both. gate JSONL... --only REGEX plans only the
journeys that match, so a filtered run is not reported MISSING for the journeys it left out; rows in the files are
judged as they are. The manifest of a filtered run names its filter under plan.only.

Targets. A wheel notch or a stroke goes to the centre of the left pane in dual: the content centre falls on the one
pixel gap between the two panes (or within a pixel of it), which belongs to neither, so no view received the input.
Enter in a cycle goes to the first folder of the session fixture, found by Home alone with no Down press, because the
ui-state patch pins foldersFirst true and folders sort first, so the first folder is row 0. A window that is not this session's Flea (class com.thisisgm.flea under the launched process)
mapping during a timed sample ends the series with an error row, "an external window opened", never a miss; nothing
that Flea did not start is closed.

Loads (p7-load). The right pane carries one of: a, a fresh hard-link copy of 2,000 cold 1280x720 JPEGs, whose
thumbnails Flea writes for the rows the pane shows and for no others; b, a copy of a large file to the USB stick; c,
the 5,000-entry NAS folder listing. A load is proved per sample, never per series: the probe (a: thumbnail files in the
cache; b: bytes copied; c: the right pane's list request count and whether its listing is in flight, read by the dualState
IPC) is read right before each timed input and right after its quiet, and the row carries load_probe and load_active
(it moved, or the load was busy at the read before). Loads a and c are renewed before every sample, a by replacing the
previous batch of linked pictures in the right folder with a batch of never-thumbnailed ones under names that sort just after its first row
(the folder keeps its size), c by changing the NAS folder's mtime, and the input is sent only after the probe moved since
the renewal; a probe that never moves is an error row, "the load did not start", never a timed row. A renewal waits out
the load's gap since the last one (a 0.3 s, c 1 s). A paced series renews after a key only when that gap has passed, so
at the 400 ms pace c renews on about every third key, and reads the probe before each key. The
gate passes a load group only when every sample's load_active is true.
"""
